    "language": "si-ta-en",
    "md5": "c7be073201a161bfe298d4d6eb2725ca",
    "chunk_size_bytes": 1513,
    "chunk_text": "The Bill imposes a microfinance identity on community credit practitioners. Women\u2019s groups created in line with the IRDP and also by UN organizations, working for over three decades to build community assets and ensure the education and well-being of women are forcibly incorporated as microfinance institutions, destroying their identity and autonomy. Despite consumer protection being the core objective, the Bill fails to introduce legal enforcement mechanisms to ensure it. Nor does the Bill establish regulations to curb profit-driven lending.\n\nThe right of communities to collectively organize credit is not a novel concept, but a fundamental tradition and a right now enshrined in the international law. The UN Declaration on the Rights of Peasants and Other People Working in Rural Areas -UNDROP- adopted on 17th of December, 2018 upholds these collective community rights while recognizing how such collective rights as well as community property or the commons, in everyday parlance, have been expropriated and are under threat, as a result of neoliberal reforms.\n\nTherefore, Sir, these community-based credit providers must be made exempt from this Bill. You can bring in some other law to try and regularize their provisions. But, certainly, their coming under this particular Microfinance and Credit Regulatory Bill is going to disenfranchise them; it is going to seriously affect the women and the extremely vulnerable people with a low-income, particularly in the war-affected North and the East."
  },
