    "language": "si-ta-en",
    "md5": "0b76cc49d472f037cffb9222f9885e46",
    "chunk_size_bytes": 1582,
    "chunk_text": "sure that the Sri Lankan entrepreneurs and exporters are very resilient. Even within the past few weeks, they have been talking about issues that could impact our country. I am happy to say that even within the period of this war, that is, during the last two weeks, we did not see any drop in tea exports. Our exporters are resilient. They have figured out various ways of exporting their tea and there has been no problem at tea auctions so far, no reduction in prices, and the exporters are doing well.\n\nAnd, in terms of the Regulations we are presenting today under the Colombo Port City Economic Commission Act, we need to understand about the asset we have. The Port City now has a great opportunity. As there is a level of risk being applied to the Middle East, we have a great opportunity to attract investments to the Port City at this point. Also, I have to tell you that six months ago, the Opposition started screaming when we reduced or changed the tax benefits of the Port City, that investments would not come. But, I can tell you that it has not been the case. Out of the 245 inquiries, only 13 have declined the new tax structure and everybody else is moving ahead. We are happy to say that, in the Port City, the inquiries for investments have now amounted to US Dollars 4.6 billion. Even with all these cries, there are investors who are looking at Sri Lanka in a very positive way. And, from primary investments, there is about US Dollars 1.4 billion and from secondary investments, there is about US Dollars 600 million that is being attracted to the Port City."
  },
