    "language": "si-ta-en",
    "md5": "24f5d6420b8a83896334dbd57ff46d5c",
    "chunk_size_bytes": 1757,
    "chunk_text": "And, in terms of the Regulations we are presenting today under the Colombo Port City Economic Commission Act, we need to understand about the asset we have. The Port City now has a great opportunity. As there is a level of risk being applied to the Middle East, we have a great opportunity to attract investments to the Port City at this point. Also, I have to tell you that six months ago, the Opposition started screaming when we reduced or changed the tax benefits of the Port City, that investments would not come. But, I can tell you that it has not been the case. Out of the 245 inquiries, only 13 have declined the new tax structure and everybody else is moving ahead. We are happy to say that, in the Port City, the inquiries for investments have now amounted to US Dollars 4.6 billion. Even with all these cries, there are investors who are looking at Sri Lanka in a very positive way. And, from primary investments, there is about US Dollars 1.4 billion and from secondary investments, there is about US Dollars 600 million that is being attracted to the Port City.\n\nchanges, what we are looking for is a positive trajectory towards the Port City and also with these changes, we hope there would be clarity and better investment facilitation.\n\nAt the same time, we are seeing is a strong requirement when we go through BoI inquiries. Now, with the new Investment Promotion Act, we are going to make it extremely clear what kind of investments are promoted in Sri Lanka and what kind of tax holidays each of those categories would get. If we take the extension of tax holidays, they could be extended up to ten years based on the investment. Those things had not been cleared over the past decade. That is why investments did not come to Sri Lanka."
