    "md5": "1d8970470f20f866cb69b8c3bfe93a3e",
    "chunk_size_bytes": 1934,
    "chunk_text": "world, except for Malaysia and Indonesia. Thank you very much for inviting us and we went there. Our President invited us. So, we should go. That is a different matter. But, what happened to the main countries in the Gulf Region? None of them came.\n\nThe Strait of Hormuz, as we all know, is a navigable area which is about two kilometres wide.\n\nSuch a big military power, such a massive global military establishment, the Americans, now have to plead with their friendly nations to come to their aid to get the Strait of Hormuz opened, but none of them are coming; all are refusing. Why? Because this is such a precarious position. The whole world is pushed into this position. It is a pipe dream even to get onto the Kharg Island. If they go there, they would be sitting ducks; it is so close to the Iranian coast that none of those soldiers could ever set foot there. That is the situation. So, if this situation goes on, not only would 20 per cent of the global oil supply would suffer, but if transport on Red Sea is also halted and if the Houthis start targeting the Red Sea, another 12 to 13 per cent of the world oil supply as well would be jeopardized.\n\nSo, that is the serious situation in which we ourselves are placed in. These global incidents may not have been foreseen by us, but, at least when such things happen, we must have the courage of our convictions to stand up and call a spade a spade. That had been the position of the current ruling party when they were in the Opposition. But, now, they have become great friends of the imperialists. That is why I say that when that apartheid State's horrible violations in the Gaza Strip happened, we kept silent. We did say a few words, but it was not to the satisfaction of the Palestinians then and nor is it to the satisfaction of the Iranians now. And, we are losing friends all around because of our lackadaisical Foreign Policy.\n\nHaving said that, I conclude, Sir."
  },
