    "date_str": "2026-03-20",
    "description": "Hansard of 2026-03-20",
    "url_metadata": "https://www.parliament.lk/en/business-of-parliament/hansards",
    "lang": "si-ta-en",
    "url_pdf": "https://www.parliament.lk/uploads/businessdocs/english/23396_english_2026-03-20.pdf",
    "chunk_id": "2026-03-20-2026-03-20-0054",
    "chunk_index": 54,
    "language": "si-ta-en",
    "md5": "1440dc0aae1ec6c4bd2ddf002965522f",
    "chunk_size_bytes": 1425,
    "chunk_text": "And, one last matter I would like to add, Hon. Minister, is the issue of political prisoners. The previous Government under President Ranil Wickremesinghe used to engage with us and even though we did not accept the TRC, they, at least, had a proposal. When we demanded the release of political prisoners, they released, at least, one or two. Actually, President Ranil Wickremesinghe released a few. But, you all have not released any. You are not even agreeing that there are political prisoners.\n\nSo, in light of this situation, Hon. Minister, I would like to have answers to my Question.\n\nThank you.\n\n1. The Government has acknowledged that a significant number of persons remain unaccounted for following the final stages of the armed conflict in 2009. Official figures have varied over time, reflecting different sources and methodologies such as Presidential Commissions of Inquiry, the Lessons Learnt and Reconciliation Commission and the Office on Missing Persons, which only\n\nstarted in 2018, a number of years after the war. So, we may not necessarily agree with the number here, but according to the records, this is all I have. I do not know whether it is right or wrong and whether anyone would agree that they are it is right or wrong. But, I can only give you what is on record from 2009. So, the number I can come up with is, 2,764. That could be debated, which I would not go against in the case of a debate."
  },
  {
    "doc_type": "lk_hansard_2020s",
    "doc_id": "2026-03-20-2026-03-20",
