    "lang": "si-ta-en",
    "url_pdf": "https://www.parliament.lk/uploads/businessdocs/english/23396_english_2026-03-20.pdf",
    "chunk_id": "2026-03-20-2026-03-20-0055",
    "chunk_index": 55,
    "language": "si-ta-en",
    "md5": "8f044b251022ba856df4b87c2d1e6bed",
    "chunk_size_bytes": 1960,
    "chunk_text": "started in 2018, a number of years after the war. So, we may not necessarily agree with the number here, but according to the records, this is all I have. I do not know whether it is right or wrong and whether anyone would agree that they are it is right or wrong. But, I can only give you what is on record from 2009. So, the number I can come up with is, 2,764. That could be debated, which I would not go against in the case of a debate.\n\n\u2022 Information from different sources has been consolidated and entered into a temporary database to ensure that existing records are preserved and accessible.\n\n\u2022 A formal proposal has been developed for the establishment of a comprehensive Database Management System - DBMS - to serve as the long-term solution.\n\nIn these instances, we might get help from our international friends to assist us with that.\n\n\u2022 A requirement identification process with relevant stakeholders is currently underway to ensure that the system is designed to meet humanitarian, legal and accountability needs.\n\n\u2022 In parallel, the Office on Missing Persons is being strengthened to centralize data collection, coordinate with law enforcement and civil registries and introduce digital platforms to improve transparency and accuracy.\n\n\u2022 Engagement with international partners and civil society continues to ensure methodological rigor and public trust in the process.\n\nI must say that I myself was involved with Troika as well as the UN Resident Coordinator on Human Rights. The moment we develop these proposals, they would be given to them, and we are working on them.\n\n3. About the Chemmani mass graves, I am glad that you asked because there is a lot of misinformation doing in the media, saying that the Government is not releasing enough funds or things of that nature, which are not correct at all. Even last time, we had released Rs. 19 million and as you are aware, the\n\n2523 2524\n\n\u0db4\u0dcf\u0dbb\u0dca\u0dbd\u0dd2\u0d9c\u0dda\u0db1\u0dca\u0dad\u0dad\u0dd4\u0dc0"
  },
  {
    "doc_type": "lk_hansard_2020s",
    "doc_id": "2026-03-20-2026-03-20",
    "num": "2026-03-20",
    "date_str": "2026-03-20",
    "description": "Hansard of 2026-03-20",
    "url_metadata": "https://www.parliament.lk/en/business-of-parliament/hansards",
    "lang": "si-ta-en",
    "url_pdf": "https://www.parliament.lk/uploads/businessdocs/english/23396_english_2026-03-20.pdf",
    "chunk_id": "2026-03-20-2026-03-20-0056",
    "chunk_index": 56,
    "language": "si-ta-en",
    "md5": "bb006d05b5bf3512e3d3221d5bde1189",
    "chunk_size_bytes": 1878,
    "chunk_text": "3. About the Chemmani mass graves, I am glad that you asked because there is a lot of misinformation doing in the media, saying that the Government is not releasing enough funds or things of that nature, which are not correct at all. Even last time, we had released Rs. 19 million and as you are aware, the\n\n2523 2524\n\n\u0db4\u0dcf\u0dbb\u0dca\u0dbd\u0dd2\u0d9c\u0dda\u0db1\u0dca\u0dad\u0dad\u0dd4\u0dc0\n\nLet me add something else which is not here. That is, we are also trying to prepare a timeline to bring a closure to this, and once the remains are gathered, the court will direct us to send them for forensic investigations. Some investigations are being done for identification - a public identification process went through - and I will give you more updates as they come.\n\n4 & 5. I will respond to the questions collectively since\n\nboth are similar.\n\nThe Office on Missing Persons has reported information relating to 19 mass grave sites and it is currently working closely with eight sites located in Colombo, Mullaitivu, Mannar, Batticaloa and Trincomalee. Overall, the OMP plays a critical role in advancing its mandate by expanding its operations. For this, technical support was obtained from the ICRC through workshops on forensic archaeology and engaging in training more than 40 excavation officers from the Department of Archaeology, who were trained on mass grave investigations. An extension of the workshop on advanced forensic archaeology was also organized with 60 participants from the Department of Archaeology. This training and technical support was provided to the expert team in order to create a pool of Excavation Officers to investigate the mass graves and to accelerate the process in finding out the fate of the missing persons. The OMP is working closely with the ICRC and there are commitments established to work collaboratively for the next three years. So, we are working with them."
  },
