    "chunk_size_bytes": 1588,
    "chunk_text": "to repeat. However, we have to accept the fact that during a period of war, people are made to disappear by both sides. It is not right, but wrong, and we stand against it. But, that is the nature of war. However, we do not justify it.\n\n7. The question is with regard to the Sri Lanka Accountability Project - SLAP. As far as that is concerned -that came up in January and it is a unilateral publication on the part of the UN - soon, a statement will be made by the UN Permanent Representative to Geneva.\n\nBut, my response for the time being is this. Sri Lanka has not accepted the Sri Lanka Accountability Project as it constitutes an external initiative that is counterproductive to the ongoing domestic reconciliation processes. Therefore, the Government will not engage with that external mechanism.\n\nAddressing the 60th Session of the Human Rights Council in September 2025, the Hon. Minister of Foreign Affairs, Foreign Employment and Tourism stated, \u201cThe Government is opposed to any external mechanism imposed on us such as the SLAP, which will only serve to create divisions, thereby jeopardizing the genuine and tangible national processes that have already been set in motion.\u201d\n\nAlso, if I add a few things - this will be officially communicated in time to come - how they have unilaterally gathered facts and the methodologies used are something that we cannot accept, and we have maintained it to be that way.\n\n8. Since assuming office, the Government has supported and strengthened the work of the Office on Missing Persons - OMP- resulting in several tangible outcomes:"
  },
