    "description": "Hansard of 2026-03-20",
    "url_metadata": "https://www.parliament.lk/en/business-of-parliament/hansards",
    "lang": "si-ta-en",
    "url_pdf": "https://www.parliament.lk/uploads/businessdocs/english/23396_english_2026-03-20.pdf",
    "chunk_id": "2026-03-20-2026-03-20-0150",
    "chunk_index": 150,
    "language": "si-ta-en",
    "md5": "b7d2301cbd99a67a32854fda236237f8",
    "chunk_size_bytes": 1575,
    "chunk_text": "Sir, today, the world itself is passing through a period of serious uncertainty. The conflict in the Middle East is not a distant event for Sri Lanka. Energy prices have been affected; shipping routes have been affected; air travel has been affected and tourism is also affected. Global markets have became unstable than ever before. Most importantly, thousands of Sri Lankans working overseas have been directly affected. Foreign employment is one of the strongest pillars of our economy. Remittances from our migrant workers have helped this country survive during the most difficult times.\n\nSir, Sri Lanka, as a maritime nation, is located close to one of the most important sea lanes in the world. A large portion of global trade passes through close to our shores. Because of that, any conflict in the region affects our economy directly. I repeat, because of that, any conflicts in the region affect our economy directly.\n\nUnder international law, including the law of the sea, Sri Lanka has sovereign rights within its maritime zones, but we also have a responsibility to support the safety of international sea lanes, on which our economy depends.\n\nAs a country located near major energy and shipping routes, any instability in those waters could directly affect our fuel supply, trade and economic stability. For that reason, Sri Lanka supports the freedom of navigation, the security of maritime transport and peaceful use of oceans in accordance with the international laws. For small nations, these rules are not theoretical, but are essential for their survival."
  },
